<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Fitna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/656">656</a>–<a href="/years/661">661 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Fitna was the first civil war within the Muslim community, fought in 656–661 CE after the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> Uthman. It pitted Caliph Ali against opponents including Mu'awiya, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and ended with Ali's assassination and Mu'awiya's founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>. It closed the era of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> and laid the roots of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>–Shi'a division.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-fitna.jpg" alt="First Fitna" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Al Ameer son · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Fitna_Map,_Ali-Muawiya_Phase.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Under Umar, who was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> from 634, the conquests of Byzantine and Sasanian lands brought revenue to the Muslims, and provincial governors ruled with considerable autonomy. Uthman succeeded Umar after the latter's assassination in 644. He relied on his Umayyad relatives and appointed his kinsmen to nearly all the important governorships, which led to accusations of nepotism and to resentment among other members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>. As the flow of spoils slowed and he demanded that provincial surplus be forwarded to him, opposition grew, above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Dissidents from both provinces marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and killed Uthman in June 656. Ali was then recognised as caliph.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a>, Talha and Zubayr opposed Ali, demanding vengeance for Uthman and a new election. Ali defeated them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-camel">Battle of the Camel</a>, outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, on 8 December 656. Talha and Zubayr were killed, and Ali sent Aisha back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and announced a pardon. Mu'awiya, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and Uthman's cousin, whom Ali had tried to dismiss, then declared war, demanding vengeance for Uthman. The armies met at Siffin in July 657. When the balance turned towards Ali, the Syrians raised copies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> on their lances and asked for arbitration, and Ali accepted under pressure from his own army.</p><p>The arbitration, agreed on 2 August 657, went badly for Ali, and a group that rejected it, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a>, gathered at Nahrawan and declared both Ali and Mu'awiya infidels. They killed civilians who did not share their views, and Ali's forces destroyed them at the Battle of Nahrawan. In 658 Mu'awiya sent about 6,000 Syrian troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and took the province, depriving Ali of one of his wealthiest regions. A raid under Busr ibn Abi Artat then went through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, executing supporters of Ali.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In late 660 Ali and Mu'awiya agreed to stop fighting. In January 661 Ali was assassinated at the Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> by the Kharijite Abd al-Rahman ibn Muljam. His son Hasan was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in Kufa, but his forces suffered defections as Mu'awiya marched on the city with a large army. Hasan made a treaty ceding the caliphate, with a general amnesty and a provision that a council would choose the caliph after Mu'awiya's death. Mu'awiya was recognised as caliph, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Fitna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ali ibn Abi Talib’s troubled caliphate from 656 to 661 CE was dominated by the <b>First Fitna</b>, the civil war between Ali and the governor of Syria, Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan, over responsibility for Uthman’s murder.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>His murder triggered the <b>First Fitna</b>, a civil war.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-17">17 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
